    {
      "claim": "The artillery attacks on Knin, Benkovac, Obrovac and Gračac violated the principle of distinction because they were directed at towns that were not military objectives and the attackers failed to verify targets, constituting intentional attacks against civilian objects.",
      "reasoning": "Article 8 of the Rome Statute defines a war crime as \"Intentionally directing attacks against civilian objects, that is, objects which are not military objectives\". The towns in question were civilian population centres; the Trial Chamber found that the HV could not identify any legitimate military targets of opportunity in three of the four towns and that impact sites lay beyond a 200‑metre margin from any claimed target. This demonstrates a failure to distinguish between civilians and combatants, satisfying the elements of the prohibited conduct under article 8(b)(ii).",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "8",
          "quoted_text": "Intentionally directing attacks against civilian objects, that is, objects which are not military objectives;"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The artillery attacks were disproportionate because the expected incidental civilian harm was clearly excessive in relation to any concrete and direct military advantage that could be anticipated.",
      "reasoning": "Article 8 of the Rome Statute also criminalises \"Intentionally launching an attack in the knowledge that such attack will cause incidental loss of life or injury to civilians… which would be clearly excessive in relation to the concrete and direct overall military advantage anticipated\". Expert testimony in the trial established that wind, temperature and the inherent inaccuracy of the artillery used made precise targeting unlikely, yet the attacks produced widespread civilian casualties and mass displacement. The absence of feasible precautions and the scale of civilian harm therefore satisfy the proportionality element of article 8(b)(iv).",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "8",
          "quoted_text": "Intentionally launching an attack in the knowledge that such attack will cause incidental loss of life or injury to civilians or damage to civilian objects or widespread, long-term and severe damage to the natural environment which would be clearly excessive in relation to the concrete and direct overall military advantage anticipated;"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Beyond reasonable doubt, the evidence presented meets the evidentiary standard required to convict for war crimes under the Rome Statute.",
      "reasoning": "The Rome Statute obliges the Court to establish each element of a war crime beyond reasonable doubt. The Trial Chamber’s forensic analysis of impact sites, the application of a consistent 200‑metre margin of error, and corroborating expert testimony on weapon accuracy collectively provide a reliable factual basis. This factual matrix, read in conjunction with the legal standards set out in article 8, fulfills the high standard of proof, leaving no reasonable doubt that the attacks were unlawful.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "8",
          "quoted_text": "War crimes"
        }
      ]
