    {
      "claim": "The artillery bombardments of Knin, Benkovac, Obrovac and Gračac were attacks directed against civilian objects, which are prohibited under international humanitarian law.",
      "reasoning": "International humanitarian law requires parties to distinguish between military objectives and civilian objects. The Trial Chamber found that the HV could not identify any legitimate military targets in the four towns and that impact sites lay more than 200 metres from any alleged target, indicating that the attacks were not aimed at military objectives but at civilian areas. Under the Rome Statute, intentionally directing attacks against civilian objects is a war crime (art. 8(b)(ii)). Because the towns were inhabited civilian localities with no verified defended military installations, the attacks constitute a breach of the distinction principle and are therefore unlawful.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "8(2)(b)(ii)",
          "quoted_text": "Intentionally directing attacks against civilian objects, that is, objects which are not military objectives;"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The artillery attacks caused extensive destruction that was not justified by military necessity and were therefore unlawful under the prohibition of wanton destruction.",
      "reasoning": "The Rome Statute criminalises \"extensive destruction and appropriation of property, not justified by military necessity and carried out unlawfully and wantonly\" (art. 8(b)(iv)). The Chamber’s factual findings show large numbers of civilians fled the area, that impact sites were dispersed beyond a reasonable margin of error, and that the HV lacked the capacity to discriminate moving police or military vehicles. These facts demonstrate that the destruction inflicted on the towns far exceeded any concrete and direct military advantage that could be derived from the attacks, failing the proportionality test and satisfying the element of wanton, unjustified destruction.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "8(2)(b)(iv)",
          "quoted_text": "Extensive destruction and appropriation of property, not justified by military necessity and carried out unlawfully and wantonly;"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The commanders who ordered and supervised the artillery operations bear individual criminal responsibility because they had effective control, knew or should have known of the unlawful nature of the attacks, and failed to take feasible precautions to prevent them.",
      "reasoning": "Customary international humanitarian law imposes command responsibility on superiors who, having effective control over subordinates, either order the commission of war crimes or fail to prevent or punish such crimes. The factual record shows that the colonel‑general was the overall operational commander of Operation Storm in the southern Krajina, and the assistant minister of the interior commanded the special police forces that participated in the artillery strikes. Their positions afforded them effective control over the forces that fired the shells. Given the evidence that the HV could not identify legitimate targets and that the attacks were conducted without adequate precautions, the commanders either intentionally directed unlawful attacks or were reckless in permitting them, satisfying the requisite mens rea for command responsibility.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "8(2)(b)(ii)",
          "quoted_text": "Intentionally directing attacks against civilian objects, that is, objects which are not military objectives;"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

